WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.270
- Did I hear that your mom
is living with you right now

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:03.640
to help you with the kids?

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.630
- Yes.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.630
She actually retired and
became our full-time nanny

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.230
when my first, when my daughter was born,

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.670
about three and a half years ago.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.050
She's been living with us ever since.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.070
And honestly, she gets along
with my husband really well.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.130
And who better than your
mother to look after your kids?

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.360
- Well, I was going to say that.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.193
How's that going though?

00:00:26.193 --> 00:00:28.153
Because I remember whenever
I was sleep training,

00:00:28.153 --> 00:00:31.810
my eldest River, my mom and sister,

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.300
like, and I don't care yeah,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.670
I'm saying it on television.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.300
They totally shamed me
for letting her cry.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.010
But the book said to let
her cry, if she's not sick,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.770
you know, you got to let them self sooth.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.180
And so I let him self sooth,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.500
and I got hardcore shamed
while playing rummikub.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.350
And I'm just saying like,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.540
parents have different
ideas than what you have.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.900
I feel like you might be siding
with my mom and my sister

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.780
on this one? (laughs)

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.150
- No, no shaming allowed.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.940
And everybody does things differently.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.800
It's like instinct kind of,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.430
kind of what you say goes

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.580
because you're the one who had the kid.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.600
- No, I know.
- Am I right?

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:05.580
- But do you not ever,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.460
so your mom and you, you raised like,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.750
y'all are doing it the same way?

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.272
Y'all never butt heads?

00:01:10.272 --> 00:01:11.240
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.320
She, listen, it's such
an education because

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.510
I'm observing her parent.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.560
Like she parented me with
my daughter and I'm like,

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.870
wait, wait, wait, you can't say that.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:25.703
Let's not say that.

00:01:25.703 --> 00:01:28.470
Can we, can we, can we maybe say it,

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.020
say it this way or
maybe not say it at all?

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.290
It's just, it's, she's a very
specific way of doing it.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:33.123
It's in her bones.

00:01:33.123 --> 00:01:36.240
She raised two kids and
I'm sort of new at it,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.910
but I have a way of doing things

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.400
that I think is a little
gentler in some ways.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.010
And when I bring it up, we
can compromise and we do,

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.950
we're very collaborative, but it is,

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.050
it is somewhat different.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.210
I mean, I was a terrible child.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.420
So that's also different.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:53.650
My daughter is not.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:54.540
- I think that's amazing though.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.540
They get to grow up
around a grandma as well.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.730
'Cause I didn't grow up with grandparents.

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.100
So that's amazing that they
get that right in the house.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.340
Yeah.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.290
- Also, they're getting,
they have three parents.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.570
We have two kids and three
adults, three parents.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.210
Who could be, I I'm,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.000
I wish they knew how
lucky they were right now.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.200
Really, I mean, I had
my grandparents close,

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:14.841
but yeah, it's different.

00:02:14.841 --> 00:02:17.780
- Well, congrats on the birth of your son.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.200
Don't you love having newborns?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.200
I love the smell.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.033
I want to eat them.

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:21.866
They're so cute.

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:25.770
- Yes, I leave the barn when
I'm working to go sniff him.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.160
- I know!
- That's it!

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.640
- They smell so good.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.890
But then they cry a lot sometimes.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:32.720
- It's, the newborn phase is tough.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.280
I don't, I'm not into it.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.880
I've never, I wasn't
into it the first time.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.050
I'm not into it now.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.600
Yes, he smells good,

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.440
but I'm also terrified
to go to bed every night

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.600
because I'm afraid, he's just,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.040
something bad's going to happen.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:43.873
Knock on wood.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.900
So yeah, no, I just I'm really
happy with three and a half.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.900
If babies could be born
at three and a half,

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:50.840
that would be ideal.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.470
- Like potty trained.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.580
It's like, yeah.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.180
If they could pop out potty
trained, I'd be into it.

